\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
Repeating with Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), quality estimation is comparable or improved (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy on SciERC ($\Delta$ up to $-3.4$~pp) despite 13.0~pp of oracle headroom, and indistinguishable on CoNLL (${<}0.3$~pp, 1.7~pp headroom). Within the 3B--8B range tested across two model families, task difficulty is a stronger predictor of the gap than model scale; results hold at smaller scale (LLaMA-3.2-3B, 13.6~pp headroom; Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Instance degeneracy also scales with model capability: 8B shows twice the degeneracy of 4B on SciERC (21\% vs.\ 10.4\%) despite comparable within-instance utility correlation (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{LoRA-rank ablation.}
To disentangle model capacity from degeneracy, we train Qwen3-8B on SciERC with LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ rank${=}32$ in all other experiments).
Reducing rank lowers degeneracy from 21\% to 7.2\% and increases oracle headroom from 13.3~pp to 16.0~pp, confirming that fine-tuning capacity drives output convergence.
However, selection still fails: the best signal (LP, 0.635) falls short of greedy (0.640), and all consensus-proximity signals perform worse (SJ 0.631, VC 0.592).
Quality estimation remains comparable (VC $\rho{=}0.394$ vs.\ $0.379$; SJ $\rho{=}0.365$ vs.\ $0.360$).
Low degeneracy is thus a necessary but not sufficient condition for selection---LP compression persists regardless of LoRA rank, indicating that both mechanisms must be addressed simultaneously.

\paragraph{Supervised verifier baseline.}
To test whether the information bottleneck lies in the aggregation method or the features themselves, we train a logistic regression verifier on all five signals (3-fold cross-validation on SciERC, $N{=}16$---giving the verifier access to twice as many samples as the main results).
The supervised selector achieves F1${=}0.641$, below both LP (0.647) and greedy (0.644),\footnote{Greedy F1 is 0.644 (3-seed mean in the verifier experiment), consistent with Table~\ref{tab:selection} (0.645, 2-seed mean on the gold-filtered subset).} closing none of the oracle gap despite the additional samples.
Its AUROC (0.652) is lower than single-signal VC (0.706), and feature importance is dominated by VC (coefficient 0.65--0.92) with SJ receiving a negative coefficient.
A 2-layer MLP and random forest on the same five features achieve comparable or slightly worse performance (MLP AUROC: 0.639; RF: 0.604), confirming that the bottleneck lies in the feature space rather than the aggregation model.
The failure of supervised signal combination confirms that the bottleneck is not in how signals are aggregated but in what information they encode: these features lack per-sample discriminative content sufficient for selection.

\paragraph{Task-specific decoding interactions.}
The free-form ablation reveals divergent behavior across tasks.
On RE, LP selection flips from $-0.68$~pp (constrained) to $+1.69$~pp (free-form; preliminary, single RE benchmark), a reversal that spans all 7 relation types and is driven by selective variance reallocation ($p{<}0.0001$).
The win/loss ratio shifts from 36:59 (constrained) to 45:45 (free-form), with the gains concentrated on relation-dense instances (mean 3.3 gold relations).
This interaction does not generalize to NER, where selection remains negligible under both regimes.
This ablation uses a single seed (seed${=}42$); whether the RE reversal replicates across seeds and generalizes to other RE benchmarks warrants further investigation.
The non-tied subset ($n{=}210$) is partially reshuffled under free-form decoding: Jaccard similarity between the constrained and free-form non-tied sets is 0.472, indicating that 87 instances newly gain LP discrimination while others lose it, yielding a $3{\times}$ increase in within-instance LP range for the newly non-tied subset.
Despite this reshuffling, aggregate NER selection gains remain negligible---score compression is not a fixed property of specific instances but rather a pervasive regime that shifts across instances without producing net selection improvement.
Free-form decoding also slightly improves quality estimation (NER VC $\rho$: 0.379$\to$0.409), though this does not translate to selection gains.

